<!--
    GH-854 (kuna), the Windows arm64 convention: AARCH64_win.cspec lists x8 as its
    `hiddenret` input entry exactly as the Linux AARCH64.cspec does, so a value a
    caller leaves in x8 is printed as the next call's first argument there too.
    gh854-hiddenretarg-a64.xml carries the full fixture and the reasoning; this
    test runs three of its functions under this compiler spec.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 "active-input-trial-scoring", P0 assertion
    `option hiddenretarg on|off`.

      f     mov x8,#0x38; mov x0,#3; bl foo   foo is `add x0,x0,#1; ret`
      g     mov x8,#0x38; svc #0; bl lib      lib's body proves nothing: the
                                              null-page constant alone
      use   mov x8,sp; mov w0,#7; bl mk       the control: mk stores through
                                              x8, a real hidden return

    Pass 1 (`option hiddenretarg off`) reproduces the BUG: `foo(0x38,3)` and
    `lib(0x38)`.
    Pass 2 (`option hiddenretarg on`, the shipped default) is the FIX, and mk
    keeps its result buffer in both passes.
-->
